"The surah begins in its first verse with the address “O Prophet!”, primarily emphasizing an expression of veneration (ta'zim). This address indicates that the admonition is directed not to the Prophet's person himself, but to convey a ruling that must be communicated to the ummah by virtue of his status of prophethood. The subsequent question, “Why do you make unlawful?”, carries both present and future tense meanings through its imperfect verb form (mudari'); hence, it can be understood as “why do you make unlawful” or “why will you make unlawful.” Since the interrogative particle conveys an element of disapproval/denial (inkar), the verb actually signifies prohibition (nahy); that is, it means “do not do so, it is not good.”\n\nThe concept of “tahrim” (making unlawful) in the verse can be creedal, verbal, or practical. To deem what Allah has made unlawful as lawful, or what He has made lawful as unlawful, is unbelief (kufr) or a prohibited act; this also applies to the Prophet. The tahrim meant here is in the nature of an oath, whether practical or verbal. According to the Hanafi school, saying “may what is lawful be unlawful to me” is considered an oath; depending on the intention, the ruling of ila, zihar, or talaq (divorce) is applied. Al-Shafi'i, however, did not consider tahrim to be an oath, stating only that it requires expiation (kaffarah) with regard to wives. Different opinions have been transmitted from the Companions and the Successors (tabi'un).\n\nIn the second verse, the expression “Allah has ordained for you” appears. While the word “fard” here carries the meaning of an absolute obligation when used with “'ala”, when it comes with “lam” it means both “to ordain” and “to explain/clarify.” Muqatil interpreted this verse as “Allah has explained,” while others said “He has ordained”; both meanings are correct. Next, the word “tahillah” (tahlil) is explained: The lawful dissolution of an oath occurs either by fulfilling it properly, or by means of an exception (saying insha'Allah), or by breaking it and offering expiation. Although the Prophet's tahrim was related to an oath, the verse commands not the breaking of an oath, but its preservation. However, in oaths that are harmful, it is more virtuous to offer expiation and break them.\n\nIn the third verse, it is recounted that the Prophet confided a secret to one of his wives (Hafsah), and she disclosed this secret to another wife (Aishah). Allah informed His Prophet of this situation through revelation, and the Prophet confronted his wife with part of the secret while refraining from reproaching her with another part. Reports differ as to what this secret was: The most authentic report mentions the oath regarding honey syrup, another mentions the tahrim of Mariyah, and yet another mentions the glad tiding of the caliphate of Abu Bakr and Umar. Although harmonizing these reports does not seem possible, commentators of the Quran state that the primary incident was the Prophet's making ila from his wives (a month of seclusion) and the events that unfolded during this period.\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, it is emphasized that the Prophet sought the pleasure of his wives in the face of this incident. However, Allah informs His Prophet that it is not appropriate for him to make unlawful what He has made lawful for him merely to please his wives. This admonition is not a condemnation of the Prophet, but a cautionary reminder (tanbih) pointing to the loftiness of his station. Following this, the sentence “Allah is Oft-Forgiving, Most Merciful” serves to alleviate the Prophet's sorrow and provide consolation. In conclusion, these verses offer important lessons regarding the safeguarding of family secrets, the importance of trust between husband and wife, and the responsibilities of the Prophet's wives.\n\nIn the commentary on the 4th verse, it is stated addressing Aishah and Hafsah, the wives of the Prophet (peace be upon him), that your hearts have swerved, meaning you fell short in sincerity toward the Prophet. If you repent, your hearts will be set right. Here, the word 'saghat' means inclining to one side. Commentators have stated that the particle 'fa' in the sentence indicates causation, and that the inclination is a deviation from the truth. Furthermore, the plural usage in the phrase 'your hearts' points to the Prophet's wives grouped into two sides: Aishah and Hafsah on one side, and Zaynab and the others on the other side.\n\nIn the 5th verse, the address is directed to all wives: 'Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you, will give him wives in your place better than you.' The attributes of these better wives are listed as follows: Submitting to Allah (muslima), believing (mu'mina), steadfastly obedient (qanita), repenting (ta'iba), worshipping ('abida), fasting (saiha), previously married and virgins. The word 'sahat' (saihat) is a metonymy for fasting; for a fasting person resembles a traveler who has no provisions with them. The distinction between previously married and virgins recalls that among the Prophet's wives, only Aishah was taken as a virgin.\n\nThe 6th verse addresses all believers: 'O you who have believed! Protect yourselves and your families from a Fire.' The head of the family is responsible for directing his household toward Allah's commands. The fuel of that Hellfire is people and stones; over it are assigned harsh angels (zabaniyah) who do not disobey Allah's command. In the 7th verse, it is addressed to the disbelievers: 'Make no excuses today; you are only being recompensed for what you used to do.' This is in the context of encouraging believers to repent and orienting the Prophet against the disbelievers.\n\nIn the 8th verse, sincere repentance (tawbah nasuh) is commanded. The word 'nasuh' means either 'most pure and sincere' or 'most rectifying.' Nasuh repentance is to abandon sin purely because of its ugliness and to firmly resolve never to return to it. In the hadith, it is described as 'not returning to sin just as milk does not return to the udder.' According to Ali, there are six conditions of repentance: remorse, making up missed obligations, returning rights to their owners, seeking mutual forgiveness and clearing liabilities, firmly resolving never to return, and melting the ego through obedience. Allah conceals the sins of those who repent and admits them into Paradise; on that day, the light of the believers will run before them and on their right.\n\nThe 9th verse: 'O Prophet! Strive against the disbelievers and the hypocrites, and be harsh with them.' This verse is a warning against those who spread false rumors and caused corruption during the tahrim incident. While jihad against hypocrites is carried out by presenting proof and conveying the message, armed jihad is also commanded against disbelievers. In the 10th verse, Allah sets forth the wives of Noah and Lot as an example for those who disbelieve. Even though these two women were under the marriage bond of righteous prophets, they betrayed them; they disbelieved and leaked revelation secrets to the enemies. The betrayal here is hypocrisy in religion, not adultery.\n\nIn the 11th and 12th verses, good examples are presented to the believers: Asiya, the wife of Pharaoh, prayed, 'My Lord! Build for me a house near You in Paradise, and save me from Pharaoh and the wrongdoers.' As for Mary (Maryam), she guarded her chastity, Allah breathed into her from His spirit, and she testified to the truth of the scriptures. These examples demonstrate that believing women can remain on the straight path even in an evil environment.\n\nThe wives of the prophets Noah and Lot failed to possess the faith and obedience demanded by the honor of being their wives; they inclined toward disbelief and ingratitude, causing harm to their husbands. They even betrayed their trust by conveying secret intelligence to the enemies of Allah. For this reason, they incurred the wrath of Allah, and not even their prophet husbands could save them from the punishment. This is a cautionary lesson for the disbelievers; everyone is accountable for their own deeds.\n\nFor those who believe, Allah gave Asiya, the wife of Pharaoh, as an example. Despite Pharaoh's tyranny, Asiya believed and asked Allah to build for her a house in Paradise and to deliver her from Pharaoh and his evil deeds. Allah accepted her prayer, took her soul as a martyr, and raised her to the high stations of Paradise. This is glad tidings for righteous women who preserve their faith despite having a wicked husband.\n\nAnother example is Mary, the daughter of Imran. She guarded her chastity, and through the breathing of Allah's spirit, she became pregnant with Jesus. Mary affirmed the words of her Lord and His scriptures, persevered in worship, and was counted among the qanitin (the obedient). This shows that she attained an exalted station combining both female and male qualities.\n\nThe use of the pronouns in this ayah indicates that the Arabic word 'farj' is used metonymically (kinayah) and that the act of breathing was from above. Mary's preservation of her chastity and her belief in the words of Allah served as the means for her conceiving Jesus in an extraordinary manner. This is a miracle demonstrating Allah's power.\n\nWhile Allah condemned the wives of Noah and Lot to Hell on account of their disbelief, He exalted Asiyah and Mary because of their faith and chastity. These examples are an instructive lesson for the believers and their families. Everyone will be recompensed according to their own deeds. This surah serves as a complement to Surah at-Talaq regarding family law and moral upbringing, and prepares the transition to Surah al-Mulk."